course: world
lesson: 61
title: Rocks, minerals and fossils
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Rocks are made of minerals, natural solid materials each built from the same tiny particles arranged in a regular pattern; granite, for example, is a mix of grains of quartz, feldspar and mica that you can see with a magnifying glass.
explain: Rocks form in three main ways. Igneous rock forms when melted rock cools and hardens; sedimentary rock forms when layers of sand, mud or shells pile up under water and are pressed and cemented together; metamorphic rock forms when old rock is squeezed and heated deep underground until it changes without melting.
explain: Rocks are slowly recycled in the rock cycle: they are broken by weathering, carried away as sand and mud, laid down in layers, buried, heated, and sometimes melted and pushed up again, over millions of years.
explain: A fossil is the remains or trace of a living thing from long ago kept in rock. Most fossils form when a dead plant or animal is quickly buried in mud or sand; its hard parts are slowly replaced by minerals and turn to stone, so fossils are found mostly in sedimentary rock.
explain: For example, a footprint left by a dinosaur in soft mud, filled with sand and hardened, can still be seen today, more than 66 million years later.
fact: Rocks are made of one or more minerals.
fact: Igneous rock forms when magma or lava cools and becomes solid; granite and basalt are igneous rocks.
fact: Sedimentary rock forms from layers of sand, mud or shells pressed together; sandstone, limestone and chalk are sedimentary rocks.
fact: Metamorphic rock forms when rock is changed by great heat and pressure without melting; marble is limestone changed in this way.
fact: Lava that cools quickly makes rock with tiny crystals or none, and magma that cools slowly underground makes big crystals.
fact: Lava full of gas bubbles can cool quickly into rock with many small holes, such as pumice.
fact: In layers of sedimentary rock that have not been turned over, the lower layers are usually older than the upper ones.
fact: A fossil is the remains or trace of a living thing from long ago preserved in rock.
fact: Fossils are found mostly in sedimentary rock, because living things are buried in mud and sand.
fact: Soft body parts usually rot away before they can be buried, so fossils of soft animals are rare.
fact: Hardness of minerals is compared by scratching: a harder mineral scratches a softer one; diamond is the hardest natural mineral.
fact: Chalk is made mostly of the tiny shells of sea creatures that lived millions of years ago.
fact: Coal formed from plants of ancient swamps buried and pressed for millions of years.
exercise: What are rocks made of? | One or more minerals | Hardened soil and plant roots | Frozen water and sand | Only one kind of crystal each
exercise: How does igneous rock form? | Melted rock cools and becomes solid | Layers of mud are pressed together over many years | Old rock is heated deep down without melting | Shells pile up on the sea floor and harden
exercise: How does sedimentary rock form? | Layers of sand, mud or shells are pressed and cemented together | Lava from a volcano cools on the surface | Rock is squeezed and heated deep underground until it slowly changes | Crystals grow inside a cave from dripping water
exercise: Which rock is metamorphic? | Marble | Sandstone | Chalk | Basalt
exercise: What is marble made from? | Limestone changed by heat and pressure | Lava that cooled very slowly deep inside a volcano | Sand pressed together under a lake for many years | Ice that froze so hard it turned into stone
exercise: Which rock is igneous? | Granite | Limestone | Chalk | Sandstone
exercise: In which kind of rock are most fossils found? | Sedimentary rock | Igneous rock | Metamorphic rock | All kinds equally often
exercise: Why are fossils rare in igneous rock? | Living things would burn up in melted rock | Igneous rock is too young to hold fossils | Animals never lived anywhere near volcanoes | Igneous rock is too hard to dig into
exercise: How do most fossils form? | A living thing is buried quickly and its hard parts slowly turn to stone | A living thing freezes in cold rock and stays as it was | A living thing is pressed flat by a falling rock in a single day and stays that way | A living thing slowly turns into a crystal on its own
exercise: What is the hardest natural mineral? | Diamond | Quartz | Granite | Gold
exercise: How can you tell which of two minerals is harder? | The harder one scratches the softer one | The harder one is always the heavier of the two | The harder one is always darker in colour | The harder one always shines more in the light
exercise: What is chalk made of mostly? | Tiny shells of ancient sea creatures | Ash from old volcanoes | Crushed bones of dinosaurs and other big animals | White sand blown by the wind
exercise: What did coal form from? | Plants of ancient swamps pressed for millions of years | Lava that cooled in the dark underground | Dinosaur bones buried in deserts | Black mud washed down by rivers
exercise: Why does granite have big crystals? | Its magma cooled slowly deep underground | It was pressed hard under the sea for millions of years | Its lava cooled fast in the air above a volcano | Its grains were glued together by salty sea water
exercise: What is the rock cycle? | The slow change of rocks from one kind into another over millions of years | The rolling of stones down a hill after rain | The yearly melting of rocks in summer | The way rocks spin as the Earth turns
test: A cliff shows many flat layers of rock, and the layer at the bottom holds fossils of fish. Which is most likely true? | The bottom layer is older than the layers above it | The top layer is the oldest one | All the layers formed together in a single day of flooding | The fish swam into the rock after it hardened
test: A rock with no visible crystals and many small holes is found near a volcano. How did it most likely form? | Lava full of gas cooled quickly on the surface | Mud dried slowly at the bottom of a lake | Limestone was heated deep underground | Shells were pressed together on the sea floor
test: Which is true about fossils? | They are remains or traces of living things preserved in rock | They are stones that look like animals by chance | They are bones that have stayed exactly as they were | They form only in ice and never in rock
test: A builder wants a stone that started as limestone and was changed by heat and pressure. Which should he ask for? | Marble | Granite | Sandstone | Basalt
test: You scratch a mineral with a steel nail and the nail leaves no mark, but the mineral scratches the nail. What do you learn? | The mineral is harder than the steel | The mineral is softer than the steel | The mineral must be diamond | Hardness cannot be tested this way
test: What would happen to a sandstone buried very deep and heated for millions of years without melting? | It would change into a metamorphic rock | It would turn back into loose sand | It would become an igneous rock at once | It would stay exactly the same forever
test: Why is it very lucky to find a fossil of a soft jellyfish? | Soft bodies usually rot away before they can be buried and turned to stone | Jellyfish lived only for a very short time on Earth | Jellyfish lived only in the deepest sea, where nothing can ever be buried in mud | Jellyfish never die, so they leave no bodies
test: A footprint of a dinosaur is found in stone. What kind of fossil is it? | A trace fossil left by the animal | A part of the animal's body turned to stone | A crystal shaped like a foot by chance | A drawing carved by early people
test: Where does the sand in sandstone come from? | Older rocks broken down by weathering | Ash thrown out by volcanoes and nothing else | Salt left behind when old seas dried up | Dust that falls to Earth from outer space
test: found a shell shape in a rock on the beach, how'd it even get in there | The shell was buried in mud long ago and slowly turned to stone | Someone carved it there to look like a real shell | The rock grew around the shell while the animal inside was still alive | Sea water melted the rock and the shell sank into it
test: wait so how come marble's all smooth and swirly, what's it made from | Limestone changed by great heat and pressure underground | Lava that cooled very slowly inside a volcano | Sand and mud pressed together under a lake | Hard clay baked in the sun for many years
test: Leo says fossils are just old bones that stayed the same for millions of years. true? | No, most fossils are remains slowly replaced by minerals and turned to stone | Yes, fossils are bones that never changed at all | Yes, but only bones kept frozen in ice | No, fossils are only ordinary stones that happen to look like animals by chance
test: my brother reckons granite's made from sand stuck together. right? | No, granite is igneous rock formed when magma cooled slowly underground | Yes, granite is sand pressed together under the sea | Yes, granite is shells and sand glued by mud | No, granite is limestone changed by heat
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the formation of igneous rock? | It forms when magma or lava cools and becomes solid | It forms from layers of sand and mud pressed together | It forms when rock is altered by heat without melting | It forms from the shells of sea creatures
exam: Identify the type of rock in which fossils are most commonly found, together with the reason therefor. | Sedimentary rock, since living things are buried in mud and sand | Igneous rock, since lava preserves living things | Metamorphic rock, since great heat and pressure keep the remains safe | Igneous rock, since it forms beneath the sea
exam: It is given that the layers of sedimentary rock have not been overturned. Ascertain which layers are in general the oldest. | The lowest layers | The uppermost layers | The middle layers | All layers are of equal age
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of marble. | It is limestone altered by great heat and pressure | It is lava which cooled rapidly upon the surface | It is sand compressed beneath a river | It is formed from ancient swamp plants
